[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Infectious Disease Specialist (Lead)): This member specializes in managing infections, particularly in immunocompromised patients like those with HIV. They lead the group in identifying the infectious etiology and initiating appropriate antifungal therapy.
 Member 2 (Neurologist): Provides expertise in neurological evaluation and management of central nervous system infections. They assess the patient's neurological signs and symptoms, aiding in diagnosis and treatment planning.
 Member 3 (Emergency Medicine Physician): Primarily responsible for the acute management of the patient in the emergency department, they evaluate the overall condition and initiate necessary investigations, including lumbar puncture.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Microbiologist (Lead)): This specialist works closely with laboratory findings and interprets the culture results. They guide the team on performing additional diagnostic tests based on the isolated pathogen.
 Member 2 (Clinical Pathologist): Focuses on evaluating cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis, including cellular counts and biochemical markers. They help interpret findings relevant to the diagnosis of cryptococcal meningitis.
 Member 3 (Radiologist): Assesses imaging studies (e.g., CT or MRI scans) that may be needed to evaluate for any complications such as increased intracranial pressure or structural changes in the brain.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Infectious Disease Specialist (Lead)): This member re
 Member 2 (Pharmacologist): Reviews the patient's medication regimen and any potential drug interactions, advising on appropriate antifungal and supportive therapies for the patient’s specific conditions.
 Member 3 (Palliative Care Specialist): Focuses on the patient's quality of life, addressing symptomatic relief of headache and other distressing symptoms that the patient might experience during the treatment process.


